/-! # Connectivity development

Complete affine towers now instantiate a reusable native square-system
sampler. Its exact equation/Jacobian filters select all regular roots; native
counting, enumeration, fixed-width vertices, and multivariate sign tests use
the proved original-source row and annihilator programs. The affine bridge
removes the unique zero dummy coordinate. Full regularity is equivalent to
regularity of every frozen affine stage and passes to all earlier prefixes.
Regular child stages extend parent samples with the original coordinates
retained. Under the explicit compact-slice and stage-regularity hypotheses,
every slice component reaches such a child label. A generic-direction
Jacobian proof and guarded specialization now supply qualitative integer
heights for fixed regular affine slices, avoiding the old height span.
Homogeneous leading cones now admit bounded integer directions preserving
transverse affine-height flags over every coefficient-field extension. A
multiplier-power system excludes nonzero roots at infinity; Macaulay minors
give polynomial bit bounds. Pure-power leading forms supply the initial
empty flag. For affine slices regular over coefficient-field extensions,
a new projective system now also excludes finite singular critical roots
with a polynomial integer-objective bit bound. It retains the original
Jacobian while taking multiplier powers, including zero multipliers. Finite
families have one common direction, with only their count's bit bound added.
The same objective extends the leading-cone flag. Native fixed-width records
charge the actual regular-parent count using source dimension, degree, and
depth. A shared-circuit PSPACE test detects singular complex child roots at
real parents with polynomial guards, including positive-dimensional fibers.
Native shared right-inverse circuits encode nonsingular parent matrices;
complex inverse witnesses select exactly the nonsingular real matrices.
A native combined writer computes the original parent Jacobian from its
shared row program and retains parent equations at the same assignment.
Native circuit relocation places the original source after a parent prefix
with exact source, marking, and child-derivative identities.
Original affine critical stages now have native shared circuits, with exact
original-row identities and completions for every root, including singular
roots and zero multipliers. Each derivative call copies only the original
source. A charged writer captures the actual signed normal records as shared
binary integer constants. One algebraically closed projective bad-root check
certifies affine regularity and cone exclusion over every field extension.
Native shared level families now read the actual signed records and evaluate
constant, ancestral-height, and midpoint levels in the original tower layout.
Their output references and lengths are fixed by the source dimension. Native
space-bounded concatenation emits each family without copying earlier stages.
A canonical reciprocal Gram determinant now supplies bounded integer
coordinate annihilators for all roots of affine systems without infinity
roots, including singular roots. Stagewise original cone certificates exclude
infinity roots of the entire multiplier-power tower, and every original
critical root lifts to that tower over the complex numbers. Thus both the
power tower and the complete original critical tower have finite root sets;
no affine-slice smoothness assumption is used. When coordinate annihilators
capture all roots, the existing equation-only native counter counts exactly
all system roots, with no Jacobian filter. The reciprocal determinant embeds
exactly in the existing nested determinant backend. Fixed-degree reflection
has a native coefficient reader charging the Gram reader, and the actual
power-tower Macaulay level, width, and determinant dimension have native
controls. Native mixed-degree homogenized rows now read the actual positive
power tower from the original source and charged signed records. FP exponent
division handles gradient multiplier powers, while inactive height rows stay
linear; the unique zero dummy includes depth zero. A native reciprocal row
completes the homogeneous family supplied to the Gram backend. Its native
coefficient reader composes the full-box determinant with fixed-degree
reflection, charging the original source and signed record readers throughout.
Actual power rows retain the original mass budget and their mixed degree
bounds. Binary degree and coefficient-size budgets now bound every coordinate
annihilator, which retains every field-valued root without a Jacobian condition.
The actual power tower now instantiates complete square sampling under stage
cone certificates. Nonzero coordinate annihilators retain every root. Odd
multiplier powers biject its real roots with the complete original Lagrange
roots while fixing ambient coordinates. Their native counts and existence
tests therefore include singular original roots. A regular coordinate grid
keeps the same primitive parameter and rational coordinates. Native equation
filtering of its short ordinals now gives vertices in bijection with all
captured roots, including singular roots. The original affine vertices preserve
ambient coordinates. Explicit coordinate objectives and prefix constraints now
supply native cone certificates using 0/1 direction records. Doubled coordinate
towers now carry native exact height and midpoint records. Their actual row
equations copy ancestral coordinates or place a current coordinate strictly
between ordered ancestral bounds. Complete original roots project to all prefixes and append arbitrary frozen-stage roots. Their
labels sample compact regular slice components without critical-stage Jacobian
conditions. For independent affine normals, every connected component of a
compact slice, including singular components, now contains an ancestry-checked
native critical sample. The number of these components has the native
next-stage root bound. At a component maximum, regularity gives normalized
multipliers; singularity gives zero-objective multipliers. No path connectivity
of singular components is inferred. Accepted complete child labels now index
a constructed clopen connected cover of each compact affine slice. Its overlap
tests are exact connections between the sampled points in that slice. The
generated source proves compactness; actual coordinate-prefix records prove
normal independence and the cone certificates. Thus these coordinate-slice
covers have native label validity and unary widths without supplied geometric
promises. Their sample connection tests still require recursion.
Streamed complete-child searches now decide singularity and regularity of
these entire frozen slices in native PSPACE. An accepted singularity query
also computes an actual complete child label whose spatial point is singular
in the exact parent slice. These tests use the original source readers;
regularity is not a supplied oracle.
At a terminal coordinate slice, every feasible point has a unique critical
multiplier lift, including multiple source roots. The actual next-stage cone
bound therefore bounds all slice points. Native guarded spatial comparison
now decides paths between complete child labels in these terminal slices,
with exact parent ancestry and terminal-coordinate validation.
Complete terminal child labels also construct a native accessible cover and
its clopen path saturation, ready for recursive endpoint attachments. Their
exact graph gives native PSPACE whole-slice connectivity as well, including
empty slices, with no supplied cover or adjacency program.
The varied row's stage now computes a native complete sampler for its frozen
prefix. Shifted roots project to that original prefix. Every constructed slab
branch retains precisely the same earlier blocks throughout the closed slab,
including singular endpoint limits and both retained joint sample references.
Native projection search now computes the complete frozen ancestor label from
a validated joint sample or a short branch descriptor. The decoded point is
the exact earlier blocks of the branch. The two descriptor programs return
identical complete ancestor words, independently of auxiliary witness choices.
For a varied active coordinate height in the newest stage, coarsening its
critical equations gives a finite ordinary root set. A zero objective
multiplier along a branch would force the height parameter to be constant.
The native coordinate zero tests therefore prove that every retained open
branch has nonzero objective multiplier. Every singular point of any frozen
parent's varying slice lifts to a zero-objective root of the actual power
system. Complete branch coverage excludes such a point: the entire varying
slice is regular throughout each retained open slab, without a regularity oracle.
The actual varying slices are compact. Every feasible point can now reach a
shifted critical root in its own fiber component, and its lifted power root
lies on a constructed branch with exactly the stated frozen parent. Thus
retained branches sample every component of every interior varying fiber.
Filtering this finite family by one exact original parent preserves that
sampling property. Each retained spatial branch stays in the parent's
varying slice throughout the closed slab, including both endpoint limits.
Removing the varied constraint constructs an actual compact coarser slice.
Its height-difference projection is proper, with exactly those shifted
slices as fibers. Parent branches now construct continuous sections of this
projection, extending through both endpoints and meeting every fiber
component. General constraint-line charts are constructed from the full
constraint derivative's surjectivity. Their actual source specialization
now constructs the component saturation of the original open slab. Its
pieces are clopen and path connected, have connected limits at both
endpoints, and overlap precisely when the corresponding actual critical
samples are connected in the retained sample fiber. Native recursive
sample-connection tests and endpoint covers remain required. Given the two
recursive endpoint component covers, the constructed saturation now supplies
a clopen path cover of the actual closed slab. Selecting one attachment per
branch retains an additive sum of branch and endpoint label types.
Native descriptor validation, ancestor computation, and word comparison now
give an exact ancestor filter in PSPACE. It tests the actual frozen blocks
and has the same answer at both retained branch descriptors. A native newest
height-row writer recovers exactly the original parent depth and sampler.
Its computed word now decodes to the exact original parent tuple, including
all ancestor multiplier slots. The native filter accepts precisely when
that original tuple equals the original ancestor decoded from the query.
For the constructed branch family, the decoded native parent is exactly the
parent used by the original component saturation. Native acceptance agrees
with the defining predicate of its finite parent-specific branch index.
Native ancestor readers now handle each joint copy. Endpoint-marker filters
use the endpoint copy's own prefix. For actual constructed branch endpoints,
that computed word equals the interior descriptor's ancestor word, including
at singular limits. The corresponding endpoint marker passes the native
ancestor filter with that same word.
Coarsening and the zero-objective constancy argument now allow a distinct
nonzero integer scale for each coordinate normal. Thus actual scaled slices
are regular throughout retained open slabs as well. The native doubled
midpoint records now supply independent normals and objectives, complete
component covers including singular points, and native whole-slice
singularity and regularity decisions. Their actual open slabs also have
constructed clopen component saturations, exact critical branch arcs, and
recursive attachment to singular endpoint covers. Native context conversion
now re-encodes ancestor labels in the original parent sampler while preserving
every decoded coordinate. Canonical descriptor filters select exactly the
original frozen-parent branch family, and both retained samples return the
same original-context word. Endpoint markers likewise compute their own
canonical ancestor words. At actual branch limits these equal the interior
word, and the native endpoint filter accepts that exact original parent.
Doubled coordinate records now also construct the terminal finite slice,
retain every multiple root, and provide native point reachability, whole-slice
connectivity, and clopen path covers for recursive endpoint attachment.
The next coordinate sweep now advances only the newest objective, preserving
every retained original and power equation. Its native parent-word conversion
preserves the entire original tuple; removing the newly active height recovers
exactly the original spatial slice. The swept coordinate, actual row, and
canonical branch and endpoint filters come from the source readers, and the
remaining-coordinate rank strictly decreases. Recursive endpoint covers then
attach the actual closed slab without supplied normal or objective controls.
Fixed-height and midpoint child records now retain one or two critical blocks,
freeze older level references, and preserve the original parent equations.
Their exact spatial slices add the selected height or midpoint equation,
including at singular roots. Native projected-word search recovers the full
parent tuple in the original sampler. Each child decreases the coordinate
rank, and depth plus twice the remaining rank does not increase.
Native parent filters select exactly the children of the decoded original
parent, and rank zero is exactly the existing terminal guard.
An original next-stage critical sample now constructs its actual fixed-height
child root and native word. The word retains the entire original tuple and
freezes the selected coordinate of that sample over its original parent.
Paired next-stage samplers now reuse the original coordinate annihilators and
native coefficient readers. Every pair of critical roots has a paired label,
and native projection recovers both original-context member words exactly,
including at singular roots. Computed parent-word comparison selects exactly
the pairs sharing an original ancestor tuple, including multipliers. A
validated compatible pair now constructs its actual midpoint child root,
retaining one ancestry prefix and appending the right critical block. Its
original parent and exact midpoint slice are proved. Computing its native
midpoint child word remains required for the recursive graph.
Symbolic squarefree coordinate
families now have complete finite anchored arcs off explicit finite exceptional
sets. Their auxiliary spatial regularity suffices; properness and endpoint
roots come from the original equations. Constructed value annihilators make
equation zero tests constant along a branch, so one original fiber indexes
all retained arcs on intervals outside a constructed finite exceptional set.
Nonvanishing leading coefficients give properness of coordinate grids;
monic grids are proper globally. Polynomial constant shifts of affine systems
with infinity exclusion now construct proper complete Gram coordinate grids
and original-root arcs, including singular endpoints. Their coordinate and
parameter degrees have uniform determinant bounds. Explicit integer-grid
test-value annihilators now have determinant degree bounds; their underlying
Gram construction also has coefficient-mass bounds. Their trailing coefficients
and squarefree coordinate factors construct a nonzero integer exceptional
polynomial with degree and root-count bounds. The affine-height arcs now use
this explicit polynomial. Their original endpoint fibers are finite even at
exceptional parameters. Singleton endpoint roots and the constructed arcs
give finite graphs of entire closed affine-height slabs, preserving paths and
connectedness. Incidence reduces to vertex equality and arc endpoint equality,
including isolated endpoint roots. Native value readers now start with the
original bivariate coordinate polynomials and multivariate test. They compute
homogenization and the reciprocal Gram determinant with binary degree
allowances, retaining parameter coefficients and charging all original readers.
The computed polynomial retains singular grid roots and is nonzero for exact
positive coordinate degrees. Pairwise coordinate midpoint signs identify every
arc endpoint among the finite endpoint roots, uniformly near either end.
Value relations with nonvanishing trailing coefficients make these signs
constant on retained intervals, including identically zero tests. Slab
incidence therefore agrees with midpoint matching at one retained sample.
The explicit squarefree exceptional factor constructs each scalar covering;
uniqueness of its lifts preserves coordinate equality and root order along
grid branches, including labels sharing some coordinates. Ordered transport
preserves distinct-root ranks. One fixed-width ordinal per coordinate gives
constant branch records that distinguish grid points and use n*W bits for
coordinate degrees at most 2^W, including empty grids and zero-width records.
Native affine-height coordinate readers now compute the exact reciprocal
Gram certificates from original integer rows and polynomial shifts. Their
canonical binary levels and unary widths are derived from source dimension
and binary row allowances, with both source readers charged. Infinity exclusion
makes the native determinant degree allowance exact for these coordinate
polynomials, supplying the grid degrees without an actual-degree scan.
The actual affine power tower now instantiates these readers for one varying
row; its original signed-record program supplies all integer coefficients and
the parameter shift is internal.
Uniform indexed access now supplies its entire coordinate grid, rejecting
invalid coordinates. The original source alone also supplies value readers
for its shifted equations on that grid. Both Gram determinants are internal;
the existing cone certificate proves nonvanishing, and every field-valued
grid root satisfies the computed value relation without regularity.
These same computed value polynomials now construct the exceptional factors
for complete actual-source height arcs, so the native readers and geometric
certificates agree. Their outer and parameter degrees have native binary bounds.
Both Gram stages now also have derived coefficient-height budgets. A native
lowest-nonzero-coefficient scan computes each actual trailing factor from the
original source, with no trailing-index or coefficient-height oracle.
A native complete slab exceptional polynomial now combines these factors
with all nonzero coordinate principal subresultants, detecting zero factors
in space. Binary degree and coefficient-mass budgets are derived, and its
avoidance implies the exact geometric exceptional conditions. No FP GCD-order
routine is a premise.
Native distinct-root and rational-cut counts compute point/gap cell codes,
with derived unary label widths. Every gap avoids the geometric exceptional
factors. Coordinate ordinal records distinguish original roots even at
singular exceptional fibers; their lengths are polynomially bounded from
source metadata, and they stay constant on retained branches. Effective
branch/endpoint membership and recursive coordinate metadata remain required.
Frozen endpoint copies now have native integer-row and midpoint-test readers.
Their retained equation's actual degree is computed in space and stored in a
record; it is never an FP oracle. Genuine endpoint roots give exclusion at
infinity, and continuous frozen copies stay constant even at singular roots.
Computing effective matching on the adjacent retained interval remains open.
The extended system now has context-preserving coefficient access and native
varying-height coordinate Gram readers with canonical derived controls.
Uniform indexed access supplies the complete extended coordinate grid and
rejects invalid coordinate indices.
Native midpoint-value annihilators now use this grid and the original linear
tests. They are nonzero on genuinely prepared endpoint records and retain all
grid midpoint values over every field. Both Gram stages have source-derived
height budgets, and native coefficient scans compute their trailing factors.
Prepared degree records have native validity checks. The computed midpoint
polynomials work uniformly for every pair of endpoint candidates; their
trailing factors make matching stable, and one retained sample characterizes
each actual slab endpoint edge. A second retained variable now freezes a
sample or boundary height, making the current point and both endpoint candidates
one finite integer system. Its original rows, exact degree-record validator,
coordinate annihilators, and complete short labels all have native readers.
Every actual tuple receives a label, including singular roots. Native midpoint
signs at these labels equal the original coordinate comparisons. Streamed
coordinate Thom comparisons now fix projections across labels; an all-label
scan decides the exact midpoint matching predicate in native PSPACE. At an
adjacent retained sample its answer equals actual left or right slab
attachment. Native scalar-coordinate comparisons now bind independently
encoded endpoint and sample heights to the joint tuple, preserving their
original labels. The combined adjacency validator is complete for joint
tuples in the requested gap. Checked edge queries therefore validate that
gap before deciding actual attachment. Native coordinate-order comparisons
and least-label scans now count distinct projected values in filtered
complete sample sets. Fixing just the two retained heights gives exactly
the actual current spatial fiber. These native coordinate ranks agree with
geometric finite-branch ranks; the latter are persistent and distinguish
branch points. A native fixed-width writer packages all coordinates into a
short descriptor. The assembled words now denote the geometric rank vectors:
they persist along retained branches and identify the branch across different
sample heights. Native algebraic cuts check the original slab code, and a
bounded native witness search validates short descriptor labels. The checked
branch relation is proved correct from the original equations and the computed
exceptional polynomial. Native endpoint markers retain actual points of their
stated fibers, with projected-point identity independent of auxiliary copies.
Bounded attachment queries combine the checked slab, branch descriptor, and
marker; their witness tests agree with actual left or right incidence.
The complete existential queries now decide those actual descriptor-to-marker
edges: fixed-sample descriptor identity eliminates arbitrary witness tuples,
and completeness constructs a tuple for every actual matching endpoint.
The scalar candidate polynomial retains both exceptional roots and derivative
roots; inside each open gap its roots are exactly the derivative samples.
Every endpoint point now has a marker, including isolated points with empty
neighboring fibers, using equal heights and three equal spatial copies.
Native scalar parameter records recover every parent coordinate, and child
slice coefficients, degrees, and root counts are computed over those records.
Complete point labels also supply actual named rational parents with native
validity and derivative probes. Endpoint parent names check the original frame
